Preserve Vulkan fresh cycled texture layouts when user commands are canceled.

WHY THIS IS A PATCH
    A fresh cycle backing is published immediately, but its UNDEFINED-to-default
    transition is recorded into the caller's cancelable command buffer. Discarding
    that buffer leaves the published backing in UNDEFINED while later commands
    assume its default layout. On cancellation, rebuild only those internal layout
    transitions in the same command buffer, without user copies, draws, graph
    semaphores, defragmentation or presentation. Successful submissions keep their
    existing initialization and incur no extra submission or wait.

LIFETIME AND FAILURE
    Backings and allocator regions are pinned until cleanup. Exceptional cancel
    initialization uses the original queue and a five-second fence timeout, in
    nanoseconds. An unsuccessful repair latches a device fault and refuses new
    acquisition/submission. Commands retained after failure use a separate list,
    never normal submitted-fence polling. Unproven retained faults permanently
    refuse new blocking waits, acquisition and submission. A wait entered before
    the fault cannot be retroactively bounded. Destruction abandons native objects
    before any window/resource destruction; private cleanup callbacks receive false.
    Known completed cancellation initializations are cleaned normally; DEVICE_LOST
    from a fence wait is also a retirement boundary. DEVICE_LOST from submission
    still means pending, just as a successful submission does.

WHEN IT STOPS APPLYING
    Pinned SDL commit: fa2c02bb6e21974a89ea9824bc53c9932abe5f9c.
    Applied on both private-queue option states. Re-point texture cycling, command
    cleanup/acquisition/cancellation and the device wait/destruction boundary.
    The private queue patch is based on this correctness patch. Do not edit the
    tracked SDL submodule. GPU tests: engine.render.texturecancellationgpu.

